Let's verify that initialisation creates the repository and wraps
streams.
We run a script in a subprocess to avoid polluting the test process.
Those subprocesses import [[learnlog]] explicitly inside the test
script, so they must not also inherit the venv's startup hook: a
[[.pth]] autostart import would run before the script can override
[[sys.argv]] or [[LEARNLOG_PROJECT_ROOT]], turning these tests into
order-of-import accidents instead of checks of [[initialize()]].
The [[test_env]] fixture therefore disables autostart while still
providing a Git identity so tests work even in CI environments
without a global Git config:

<<test helpers>>=
@pytest.fixture
def test_env():
    """Environment for explicit-import tests.

    These subprocess tests import ``learnlog`` from the script body, so
    they disable the startup hook and clear any leaked project-root
    override from the parent pytest process.
    """
    env = os.environ.copy()
    env["LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART"] = "1"
    env.pop("LEARNLOG_PROJECT_ROOT", None)
    env["GIT_AUTHOR_NAME"] = "Test"
    env["GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL"] = "test@test"
    env["GIT_COMMITTER_NAME"] = "Test"
    env["GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL"] = "test@test"
    return env
